# THE GENERATED DOCUMENTS ARE LF, ON EVERY MACHINE — and this file is a GATE, not a preference.
#
# ── THE MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-26 (review round 2, technical blocker B3) ─────────────────────────
#
# `plugin/README.md` and `plugin/commands/help.md` are written by
# `src/write-plugin-docs.ts`, which joins its lines with '\n', and `plugin-files.test.ts` compares
# the committed file to that generator BYTE FOR BYTE — which is the point of the check: a public
# install document that has drifted from the copy the screen renders is an instruction that does
# not work.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` at the SYSTEM level, and this repository carried no
# `.gitattributes` at all. So git wrote those files out in CRLF on every checkout, clone,
# `worktree add` and merge, while the generator kept producing LF: the gate went red on a file
# whose content had never changed, and it went red in `main` — 8.1 merges first, and 3.8 · 5.7 ·
# 5.5 · 3.6 rebase onto what it leaves behind. The branch looked green only because these files had
# been WRITTEN by the generator in this worktree and never checked OUT by git.
#
# `text eol=lf` is the whole fix: normalised to LF in the object database, and written out in LF in
# the working tree whatever `core.autocrlf` says. A test beside it asserts the shipped files carry
# no carriage return, so a machine that somehow gets one back finds a sentence instead of a puzzle.
plugin/README.md text eol=lf
plugin/commands/help.md text eol=lf
# Story 8.5 — generated by the same script, pinned for the same byte comparison.
plugin/commands/rules.md text eol=lf
